The core sticky override can be bypassed by later mapper modes, so the new stmap95 policy does not reliably enforce its intended protection. The diagnostics also misreport sticky-blocked candidates.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Apply sticky checks after mode transitions — /Users/cunxiy/Documents/research/autoresearch-eda/abc-harness/src/map/mapper/mapperMatch.c:1907-1907
  When a sticky override is recorded in one mapper mode and the mapper later revisits the same AIG in another mode, this `p->fMappingMode == s_Stmap93EmittedStickyMode` guard skips the veto entirely. A later accepted cut in mode 3 can therefore replace a mode-2 sticky override without satisfying the arrival/drive preservation checks, so stmap95 does not actually protect the target across later replacements.

- [P3] Preserve the pre-veto accepted flag in logs — /Users/cunxiy/Documents/research/autoresearch-eda/abc-harness/src/map/mapper/mapperMatch.c:1921-1921
  In the sticky-block path the branch was entered only because `fAccepted` was true, but clearing `fAccepted` here is what the later diagnostic `accepted-before` field prints, so every `sticky-block-replacement` row reports `accepted-before = 0`. That makes the CSV/logs hide that the sticky policy vetoed a cut selected by the previous comparator; keep a separate pre-veto value for diagnostics before clearing the selection.
